\title[A certified calculus for across-level scaling exponents]{A Certified Calculus for Across-Level Scaling Exponents,\\ with Application to Factoring-Cost Measurements\\ on a Single Population}

\author{Aristotle}
\date{23 August 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
We develop a rigorous inferential calculus for \emph{across-level scaling exponents}: the log--log slopes obtained by measuring a mean cost at two problem sizes and dividing by the lever arm. The motivating dataset consists of three factoring channels---trial division, Pollard's rho, and Fermat's difference-of-squares method---run on a \emph{single} population of balanced semiprimes at bit sizes $k \in \{16,20,24\}$, with reported per-$\log_2 p$ slopes $0.84$, $0.52$ and $0.50$.

Our central object is the \emph{power band}: a mean-cost curve trapped between $c_1 2^{\alpha k}$ and $c_2 2^{\alpha k}$, with the exponent asserted and the constant not. We prove an identifiability inequality---the two-point slope differs from $\alpha$ by at most $\log_2(c_2/c_1)/\dk$---show that it is attained, and prove its converse: two exponents separated by $2\sigma/\dk$ are indistinguishable to any two-level estimator inside a window of spread $2^\sigma$. Applying the calculus we obtain a pointwise slope band $s \pm s/\dk$ for a per-instance cost $a p^{s}$ on a dyadic population with no hypothesis on $a$; a constant-free cross-channel rigidity law $|\slope_{\mathrm{trial}} - 2\slope_\rho| \le 1/\dk$, sharpened by the Kantorovich inequality to $\log_2((4+3\sqrt2)/8)/\dk < 0.044/\dk$, together with a sharp one-parameter family $K(t) = (1+2^t)^2/(4\cdot 2^t)$ along the doubling ray; a general power-mean law $|t\slope_s - s\slope_t| \le st/\dk$; an exact gap-locality law for Fermat's method and the resulting exponent transfer law $\alpha_{\mathrm{Fermat}} = 2\beta_{\mathrm{gap}} - 1$; a no-go theorem showing cost truncation produces a slope deficit strictly below $1/8$ uniformly in the truncation level; and a shape-drift \emph{identity} making exponent compression equivalent to a decrease of the normalized moment, which converts the reported $0.84$ into the exact prediction $M_1(16)/M_1(24) = 2^{1.28}$.
\end{abstract}

